[{:seon.task/name "a-shard-says-what-each-test-waits-on"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the stability sweep of 2026-10-25 (notes/stability-2026-10-25.md; the owner: platform stability first). test side of the sweep (target/waits-test.md in the stability worktree): bin/test waits on its shards in index order (bin/test's wait $P[i]), so a hung shard 1 hides later shards' output; and test waits with no end beyond their answer: ts/ask's socket read of a live parked root (test/seon/test_store.clj:202), start-root!'s wait for ROOT LISTENING (167), stop-root! (207), kill-root! (178), binary-sh of a mode that parks (113), agent_test's and e2e's <!! event loops on an events tap (agent_test.clj:51,82,133,176,349,365,388; e2e.clj:81,236) that skip a dead agent's event, check.clj's await-event with no terminal event named (204,496,510,517,745,980,1156), bare @p / <!! after a synchronous step in procs_test (282-372), problems_test:176, step_test:11-20 (use the answer helper, procs_test.clj:466, or realized?). Correct: bin/test reports each shard as it ends (wait -n), and each test wait names its end: an agent's or root's death event ends an event loop (a loop that skips a death is red), a synchronous step's answer is read with realized? (no wait). The parked-everywhere case is jolt.async/a-process-whose-every-thread-parks-ends-its-waits. Clocks in test (check.clj:879,984,1001,649,1473,67,1135; lib_test.clj:50,81,84) are listed in target/waits-test.md for docs/clocks.md's owner to name or remove."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agent-test-shares-its-shards-root"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-10-27: prioritize what speeds up development. Root's analysis (tests-fast, 2026-10-27): 91 process tests are 1,940 s, 83% of suite time; the 55 seon.agent-test/in-agent tests each booted a built root (bin/root start) and dropped its main with a second ./seon process. Measured on a running root: boot 9.5 s of a 15 s test (the source import 4.8 s), attach 0.36 s (66 ms on a warm root), stop 1.3 s, drop about 1 s. Done: seon.test-store/shared-root! starts one root per shard process and root arguments on a main of its own of the process store, stopped and its main dropped at the process's end; in-agent takes forms as a function of the test's names {:ns :task :run :agent} (a fresh task namespace per test, the agent the attach answered), kills its agent, cancels its tasks (a run left :running kept the reconciler from quiet, and a later test's seon.await/await-quiet waited on it: a shard hung 70 minutes) and deletes their branches at its end; :own-root? keeps a root of its own for a test whose agent merges into main. Replaces the boot, stop and drop process per in-agent test (in-agent's drop-main! call). Deletes the starter fifo's shutdown hook: hooks run at once, and it raced the shared roots' stop, whose bin/held then refused to run (bin/root stop printed nothing) and every shard's roots outlived it; bin/held --reclaim removes a gone process's fifo at the next one's first use. Not done, and why: the brief's narrower digest (a leaf change reaching no agent test that does not name it) is not honest: seon.root/start! reaches 1,252 of 1,908 src forms and reads the tree (seon.fx/read-source imports it), and the forms an agent evaluates are strings, so an agent test's input stays the tree (binary-sh a tree reader). Follow-up: a scripted model per task (:seon.task/config) would let the :sets tests, one script each, share their shard's root too."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.test-store-test/agent-tests-in-one-process-share-its-root}}]
